"<div><div class=\" response-message\"></div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><h2>Nash Equilibria Analysis</h2>\n<p>✅ Analysis complete</p>\n<p>This analysis explores a <strong>Repeated Cournot Competition</strong> between two firms (Firm A and Firm B). In a Cournot model, firms compete on the quantity of output produced, which then determines the market price.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Part 1: Game Structure Analysis</h3>\n<p><strong>1. Game Type:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-cooperative:</strong> Each firm maximizes its own profit without a binding, enforceable contract.</li>\n<li><strong>Repeated Game:</strong> This is not a one-shot interaction. The firms play the same stage game over multiple periods ($t = 1, 2, \\dots, T$ or $\\infty$).</li>\n<li><strong>Simultaneous (within stages):</strong> In each period, firms choose their quantities simultaneously without knowing the other's choice for that specific period.</li>\n<li><strong>Information:</strong> Imperfect information within the stage (simultaneous moves), but typically <strong>perfect monitoring</strong> (each firm observes the other's past quantity choices).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Strategy Spaces:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stage Game:</strong> The strategy is the quantity $q_i \\in [0, Q_{max}]$. This is a <strong>continuous</strong> strategy space.</li>\n<li><strong>Repeated Game:</strong> A strategy is a complete plan of action specifying the quantity to produce in period $t$ based on the entire history of play up to $t-1$.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>3. Payoffs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Objective:</strong> Maximize the present value of the stream of profits: $\\sum_{t=0}^{\\infty} \\delta^t \\pi_{it}$, where $\\delta$ is the discount factor (representing the &quot;patience&quot; of the firm or the probability the game continues).</li>\n<li><strong>Payoff Function:</strong> $\\pi_i(q_i, q_j) = [P(q_i + q_j) - c]q_i$, where $P$ is the inverse demand function and $c$ is the marginal cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>4. Key Features:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Timing:</strong> Simultaneous moves in each period.</li>\n<li><strong>Incentives:</strong> There is a tension between the <strong>Collusive outcome</strong> (joint profit maximization) and the <strong>Nash incentive</strong> (unilateral deviation to increase individual profit).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>Part 2: Nash Equilibrium Analysis</h3>\n<p>In a repeated Cournot game, there are two primary types of Nash Equilibria: the repetition of the static Cournot-Nash equilibrium and the &quot;Collusive&quot; equilibrium sustained by trigger strategies.</p>\n<h4>1. The Static Cournot-Nash Equilibrium (Repeated)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Strategy Profile:</strong> Both firms produce the Cournot quantity $q_C = \\frac{a-c}{3b}$ in every period, regardless of history.</li>\n<li><strong>Why it’s a Nash Equilibrium:</strong> In any single period, if Firm B produces $q_C$, Firm A’s best response is $q_C$. Since this holds for every period, neither firm has an incentive to deviate unilaterally.</li>\n<li><strong>Classification:</strong> Pure Strategy Equilibrium.</li>\n<li><strong>Stability and Likelihood:</strong> <strong>Highly Stable.</strong> This is the &quot;default&quot; equilibrium. It does not require trust or a high discount factor. It is the unique equilibrium if the game is finitely repeated with a known end date (due to backward induction).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2. The Collusive Equilibrium (Trigger Strategy)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Strategy Profile (e.g., Grim Trigger):</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Period 1:</em> Produce the monopoly share $q_M = \\frac{a-c}{4b}$ (which is less than $q_C$).</li>\n<li><em>Period $t$:</em> Produce $q_M$ if the opponent has always produced $q_M$ in the past. If the opponent has ever deviated, produce $q_C$ forever (the &quot;punishment&quot;).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Why it’s a Nash Equilibrium:</strong> If the discount factor $\\delta$ is high enough (typically $\\delta \\ge 9/17$ in standard linear models), the long-term benefit of maintaining high profits outweighs the one-time gain from &quot;cheating&quot; and the subsequent loss during the punishment phase.</li>\n<li><strong>Classification:</strong> Pure Strategy Equilibrium (in the context of the repeated game).</li>\n<li><strong>Stability and Likelihood:</strong> <strong>Conditionally Stable.</strong> It is only an equilibrium if firms are sufficiently patient ($\\delta$ is high) and the threat of punishment is credible.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>Part 3: Discussion of Multiple Equilibria</h3>\n<p><strong>Coordination Problems:</strong>\nThe firms face a coordination problem. While both prefer the Collusive Equilibrium, they fear the other might &quot;cheat&quot; (produce more to capture more market share). Without communication, firms may default to the Static Cournot-Nash equilibrium to avoid being exploited.</p>\n<p><strong>Pareto Dominance:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>Collusive Equilibrium Pareto dominates</strong> the Static Cournot-Nash Equilibrium. Both firms earn strictly higher profits under collusion than they do in the Cournot-Nash outcome.</li>\n<li>However, the Cournot-Nash equilibrium is &quot;risk-dominant&quot; in many contexts because it does not rely on the cooperation of the other party to avoid a disastrously low payoff.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Which is most likely to occur?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>In Finitely Repeated Games:</strong> The Static Cournot-Nash equilibrium is the only Subgame Perfect Nash Equilibrium (SPNE) due to the &quot;unraveling&quot; effect (firms cheat in the last period, therefore they cheat in the second-to-last, and so on).</li>\n<li><strong>In Infinitely/Indefinitely Repeated Games:</strong> Collusion becomes highly likely if:\n<ol>\n<li>The number of firms is small (easier to monitor).</li>\n<li>The market is stable (predictable demand).</li>\n<li>The discount factor $\\delta$ is high (firms value future profits).</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Likelihood:</strong> In a duopoly, firms often move toward &quot;Tacit Collusion&quot; (the Collusive Equilibrium) because the mutual gains are significant and the monitoring of a single competitor is relatively easy.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></div><div><div class=\" completion-message response-message\"></div></div>"